{{also|Winken}}
==German==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|de|goh|winken}}, from {{der|de|gem-pro|*winkaną||to move side to side, sway}}, from {{der|de|ine-pro|*weng-||to bow, bend, arch, curve}}. Compare {{m|de|wanken}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈvɪŋkŋ̩/|/ˈvɪŋkən/|lang=de}}
* {{audio|De-winken.ogg|Audio|lang=de}}

===Verb===
{{de-verb-weak|class=3|winkt|winkte|[[gewinkt]] or [[gewunken]]}}

# to [[wave]]
# to [[beckon]]

====Conjugation====
{{de-conj-weak|wink|gewinkt|h}}

====Usage notes====
The regular weak past participle {{m|de|gewinkt}} is increasingly replaced with the now more common strong form {{m|de|gewunken}}<ref>{{cite-web
|date=March 14, 2017
|author=Jacqueline Kubczak
|author2=Matthias Mösch
|title=Gewinkt oder gewunken? — Schwache oder starke Flexion
|work=grammis 2.0
|url=http://hypermedia.ids-mannheim.de/call/public/fragen.ansicht?v_id=76
}}</ref><ref>
{{cite-web
|accessdate=January 8, 2018
|title=gewinkt,gewunken
|work=Google Books Ngram Viewer
|url=https://books.google.com/ngrams/graph?content=gewinkt%2C+gewunken&year_start=1800&year_end=2008&corpus=20&smoothing=3&share=&direct_url=t1%3B%2Cgewinkt%3B%2Cc0%3B.t1%3B%2Cgewunken%3B%2Cc0
}}</ref>. This is also true for derived verbs such as {{m|de|zuwinken}} or {{m|de|durchwinken}}.

